|----------|-----------|---------|------------|
| C# | Parallel.For | 3.26x | 41% |
| Rust | thread::scope | 4.5x | 56% |
| Go | Goroutines | 7.4x | 92% |
| C++ | OpenMP | 0.87x | N/A |
| Java | Executor | 1.05x | 13% |

================================================================================
CONCLUSIONS
================================================================================

## SUMMARY OF FINDINGS

The GPT-5 C# parallel implementation achieves solid performance:
1. TPL Parallel.For provides efficient work distribution
2. M-tile parallelization balances work and overhead
3. Deterministic behavior through disjoint writes
4. Reasonable efficiency considering .NET overhead

## PERFORMANCE VERDICT

| Metric | Result |
|--------|--------|
| Status | SUCCESS |
| Speedup | 3.26x |
| Efficiency | 41% (on 8 cores) |

## EFFICIENCY ANALYSIS

The 41% parallel efficiency is limited by:
1. .NET runtime overhead (GC, JIT compilation)
2. Memory bandwidth saturation
3. 256 synchronization barriers

## RECOMMENDATIONS FOR IMPROVEMENT

1. Use Span<T> for zero-copy array slicing
2. Consider pooled arrays to reduce allocation
3. Experiment with larger tile sizes
4. Profile with PerfView for .NET-specific bottlenecks

================================================================================
THESIS-READY STATEMENT
================================================================================

"The C# parallel GEMM implementation using the Task Parallel Library achieves
a 3.26x speedup over the sequential baseline, reaching 3.559 GFLOPs on a
1024x1024x1024 matrix multiplication. The implementation employs Parallel.For
to distribute M-dimension tiles across worker threads, with each iteration
owning disjoint rows of the output matrix C. The MaxDegreeOfParallelism is
bounded to Environment.ProcessorCount, preventing thread oversubscription.
Within each of the 256 (N,K) tile iterations, 16 M-tiles are processed in
parallel with approximately 524,000 floating-point operations per tile,
providing sufficient computational density to amortize the ~1 microsecond
Parallel.For overhead. The 41% parallel efficiency relative to ideal scaling
reflects the combined impact of .NET runtime overhead, memory bandwidth
contention, and synchronization costs. Notably, the implementation maintains
numerical determinism by preserving the K-dimension summation order within
the PartialMatmul kernel and ensuring exclusive write access to C row stripes.
This case study demonstrates that managed runtimes like .NET can achieve
competitive parallel GEMM performance when parallelization granularity is
appropriately tuned to balance work distribution against framework overhead."

